{"subjectId":"dsa-advanced","id":"trie","title":"Trie Data Structure","group":"Advanced Structures","order":20,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A trie (prefix tree) stores a set of words as a tree of characters, so that every operation costs time proportional to the <strong>length of the word</strong> — not to the number of words — and so that words sharing a prefix share a path. It is the structure behind autocomplete, spell-checkers and IP routing tables.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--def\"><p><strong>Trie</strong> — a rooted tree in which every edge is labelled with a character and every node represents the prefix spelled out by the path from the root. A flag on a node marks that this prefix is a complete word. The root represents the empty string.</p></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Trie for { geeks, geek, bad, bat, cat, cut } — \"geek\" and \"geeks\" share one path, \"ba\" is a prefix of two words</figcaption><pre><code>         (root)\n  ┌────────┼────────┐\n  b        c        g\n  │     ┌──┴──┐     │\n  a     a     u     e\n┌─┴─┐   │     │     │\nd*  t*  t*    t*    e\n                    │\n                    k*          * = isEndOfWord\n                    │\n                    s*</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Tries are the right choice when a dictionary of words must support these operations efficiently:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Search</strong> — is this exact word in the dictionary?</li><li><strong>Insert</strong> — add a word.</li><li><strong>Delete</strong> — remove a word (without disturbing words that share its prefix).</li><li><strong>Prefix search</strong> — does any word start with <code>pre</code>? List them all (autocomplete).</li><li><strong>Lexicographic ordering</strong> — a pre-order traversal visits the words in sorted order for free.</li></ol>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>Trie</th><th>Hash table</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Search</td><td>Θ(word_len)</td><td>Θ(word_len) average</td></tr><tr><td>Insert</td><td>Θ(word_len)</td><td>Θ(word_len) average</td></tr><tr><td>Delete</td><td>Θ(word_len)</td><td>Θ(word_len) average</td></tr><tr><td>Prefix search</td><td>Θ(prefix_len)</td><td>not supported</td></tr><tr><td>Lexicographic ordering</td><td>Θ(output_len)</td><td>not supported (needs a sort)</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>high — up to 26 pointers per node</td><td>low</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>A hash table matches a trie on exact-match operations, and uses far less memory. Reach for a trie only when you need <strong>prefix</strong> queries or <strong>sorted</strong> output — those are the operations a hash table simply cannot do.</p></div>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-binary-tree\"></i>Trie representation</h3>\n<p>For lowercase English words each node holds an array of 26 child pointers (index <code>ch − 'a'</code>) and a boolean <code>isEndOfWord</code>. The characters themselves are not stored in the nodes — they are implied by the array index on the way down.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>class TrieNode:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.child = [None] * 26          # child[i] → node for chr(ord('a') + i), or None\n        self.is_end_of_word = False       # True ⇔ the path from the root spells a stored word\n\nroot = TrieNode()                         # the empty trie: a root with no children\nprint(sum(c is not None for c in root.child), root.is_end_of_word)</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>0 False</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>child[26]</code></td><td>one slot per letter; <code>None</code>/<code>nullptr</code> means no word continues with that letter</td></tr><tr><td><code>isEndOfWord</code></td><td>marks a complete word — needed because <code>geek</code> must be found while <code>gee</code> must not</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Variants</div><p>Alphabets larger than 26 letters (Unicode, arbitrary bytes) use a hash map from character to child instead of a fixed array — slower per step, but the memory no longer scales with the alphabet size.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-search\"></i>Insert, search and prefix search</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Example</div><p><strong>Input:</strong> <code>insert geeks, geek, bad, bat, cat, cut; search(\"geek\")</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>true</code><br><strong>Input:</strong> <code>search(\"gee\")</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>false (a prefix, not a word)</code><br><strong>Input:</strong> <code>startsWith(\"ge\")</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>true</code></p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Idea</div><p>All three walk the same path. <strong>Insert</strong> creates the missing child at every step and marks the final node. <strong>Search</strong> stops with <code>false</code> as soon as a child is missing and, at the end, returns the <code>isEndOfWord</code> flag. <strong>Prefix search</strong> is search without the final flag check.</p></div>\n<div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">1</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Start at the root</div><div class=\"step-desc\">with the first character of the key.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">2</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Follow child[ch − 'a']</div><div class=\"step-desc\">insert: create the node if it is missing — search: return <code>false</code> if it is missing.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">3</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">At the last character</div><div class=\"step-desc\">insert: set <code>isEndOfWord = true</code> — search: return <code>isEndOfWord</code> — startsWith: return <code>true</code>.</div></div></div>\n<div class=\"tabs\"><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Python\"><figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>class TrieNode:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.child = [None] * 26\n        self.is_end_of_word = False\n\nclass Trie:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.root = TrieNode()\n\n    def insert(self, key):\n        node = self.root\n        for ch in key:\n            i = ord(ch) - ord('a')\n            if node.child[i] is None:            # extend the path where it stops\n                node.child[i] = TrieNode()\n            node = node.child[i]\n        node.is_end_of_word = True               # mark the complete word\n\n    def _walk(self, key):                        # node where `key` ends, or None\n        node = self.root\n        for ch in key:\n            node = node.child[ord(ch) - ord('a')]\n            if node is None:\n                return None\n        return node\n\n    def search(self, key):\n        node = self._walk(key)\n        return node is not None and node.is_end_of_word\n\n    def starts_with(self, prefix):\n        return self._walk(prefix) is not None\n\n    def words(self):                             # pre-order traversal → lexicographic order\n        out = []\n        def dfs(node, prefix):\n            if node.is_end_of_word:\n                out.append(prefix)\n            for i, c in enumerate(node.child):\n                if c is not None:\n                    dfs(c, prefix + chr(ord('a') + i))\n        dfs(self.root, \"\")\n        return out\n\ntrie = Trie()\nfor w in [\"geeks\", \"geek\", \"bad\", \"bat\", \"cat\", \"cut\"]:\n    trie.insert(w)\nfor w in [\"geek\", \"gee\", \"cut\", \"dog\"]:\n    print(f\"search {w}:\", \"true\" if trie.search(w) else \"false\")\nfor pre in [\"ge\", \"do\"]:\n    print(f\"startsWith {pre}:\", \"true\" if trie.starts_with(pre) else \"false\")\nprint(\"words:\", \" \".join(trie.words()))</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"C++\"><figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\nusing namespace std;\n\nstruct TrieNode {\n    TrieNode* child[26] = {};\n    bool isEndOfWord = false;\n};\n\nclass Trie {\n    TrieNode* root = new TrieNode();\n    TrieNode* walk(const string&amp; key) const {           // node where `key` ends, or nullptr\n        TrieNode* node = root;\n        for (char ch : key) {\n            node = node-&gt;child[ch - 'a'];\n            if (!node) return nullptr;\n        }\n        return node;\n    }\n    void dfs(TrieNode* node, string&amp; prefix, vector&lt;string&gt;&amp; out) const {\n        if (node-&gt;isEndOfWord) out.push_back(prefix);\n        for (int i = 0; i &lt; 26; i++)\n            if (node-&gt;child[i]) {\n                prefix.push_back('a' + i);\n                dfs(node-&gt;child[i], prefix, out);\n                prefix.pop_back();\n            }\n    }\npublic:\n    void insert(const string&amp; key) {\n        TrieNode* node = root;\n        for (char ch : key) {\n            int i = ch - 'a';\n            if (!node-&gt;child[i]) node-&gt;child[i] = new TrieNode();   // extend the path where it stops\n            node = node-&gt;child[i];\n        }\n        node-&gt;isEndOfWord = true;                                   // mark the complete word\n    }\n    bool search(const string&amp; key) const { TrieNode* n = walk(key); return n &amp;&amp; n-&gt;isEndOfWord; }\n    bool startsWith(const string&amp; prefix) const { return walk(prefix) != nullptr; }\n    vector&lt;string&gt; words() const { vector&lt;string&gt; out; string p; dfs(root, p, out); return out; }   // sorted\n};\n\nint main() {\n    Trie trie;\n    for (string w : {\"geeks\", \"geek\", \"bad\", \"bat\", \"cat\", \"cut\"}) trie.insert(w);\n    for (string w : {\"geek\", \"gee\", \"cut\", \"dog\"}) cout &lt;&lt; \"search \" &lt;&lt; w &lt;&lt; \": \" &lt;&lt; (trie.search(w) ? \"true\" : \"false\") &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    for (string p : {\"ge\", \"do\"}) cout &lt;&lt; \"startsWith \" &lt;&lt; p &lt;&lt; \": \" &lt;&lt; (trie.startsWith(p) ? \"true\" : \"false\") &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"words:\";\n    for (const string&amp; w : trie.words()) cout &lt;&lt; \" \" &lt;&lt; w;\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Java\"><figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.List;\n\npublic class Main {\n    static class TrieNode {\n        TrieNode[] child = new TrieNode[26];\n        boolean isEndOfWord;\n    }\n\n    static class Trie {\n        TrieNode root = new TrieNode();\n\n        void insert(String key) {\n            TrieNode node = root;\n            for (char ch : key.toCharArray()) {\n                int i = ch - 'a';\n                if (node.child[i] == null) node.child[i] = new TrieNode();   // extend the path where it stops\n                node = node.child[i];\n            }\n            node.isEndOfWord = true;                                          // mark the complete word\n        }\n        TrieNode walk(String key) {                                           // node where key ends, or null\n            TrieNode node = root;\n            for (char ch : key.toCharArray()) {\n                node = node.child[ch - 'a'];\n                if (node == null) return null;\n            }\n            return node;\n        }\n        boolean search(String key) { TrieNode n = walk(key); return n != null &amp;&amp; n.isEndOfWord; }\n        boolean startsWith(String prefix) { return walk(prefix) != null; }\n        List&lt;String&gt; words() { List&lt;String&gt; out = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;(); dfs(root, new StringBuilder(), out); return out; }\n        void dfs(TrieNode node, StringBuilder prefix, List&lt;String&gt; out) {     // pre-order → sorted\n            if (node.isEndOfWord) out.add(prefix.toString());\n            for (int i = 0; i &lt; 26; i++)\n                if (node.child[i] != null) {\n                    prefix.append((char) ('a' + i));\n                    dfs(node.child[i], prefix, out);\n                    prefix.setLength(prefix.length() - 1);\n                }\n        }\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Trie trie = new Trie();\n        for (String w : new String[]{\"geeks\", \"geek\", \"bad\", \"bat\", \"cat\", \"cut\"}) trie.insert(w);\n        for (String w : new String[]{\"geek\", \"gee\", \"cut\", \"dog\"}) System.out.println(\"search \" + w + \": \" + trie.search(w));\n        for (String p : new String[]{\"ge\", \"do\"}) System.out.println(\"startsWith \" + p + \": \" + trie.startsWith(p));\n        System.out.println(\"words: \" + String.join(\" \", trie.words()));\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"JavaScript\"><figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>class TrieNode {\n    constructor() { this.child = new Array(26).fill(null); this.isEndOfWord = false; }\n}\n\nclass Trie {\n    constructor() { this.root = new TrieNode(); }\n    insert(key) {\n        let node = this.root;\n        for (const ch of key) {\n            const i = ch.charCodeAt(0) - 97;\n            if (node.child[i] === null) node.child[i] = new TrieNode();   // extend the path where it stops\n            node = node.child[i];\n        }\n        node.isEndOfWord = true;                                           // mark the complete word\n    }\n    walk(key) {                                                            // node where key ends, or null\n        let node = this.root;\n        for (const ch of key) {\n            node = node.child[ch.charCodeAt(0) - 97];\n            if (node === null) return null;\n        }\n        return node;\n    }\n    search(key) { const n = this.walk(key); return n !== null &amp;&amp; n.isEndOfWord; }\n    startsWith(prefix) { return this.walk(prefix) !== null; }\n    words() {                                                              // pre-order → sorted\n        const out = [];\n        const dfs = (node, prefix) =&gt; {\n            if (node.isEndOfWord) out.push(prefix);\n            node.child.forEach((c, i) =&gt; { if (c !== null) dfs(c, prefix + String.fromCharCode(97 + i)); });\n        };\n        dfs(this.root, \"\");\n        return out;\n    }\n}\n\nconst trie = new Trie();\nfor (const w of [\"geeks\", \"geek\", \"bad\", \"bat\", \"cat\", \"cut\"]) trie.insert(w);\nfor (const w of [\"geek\", \"gee\", \"cut\", \"dog\"]) console.log(`search ${w}:`, trie.search(w));\nfor (const p of [\"ge\", \"do\"]) console.log(`startsWith ${p}:`, trie.startsWith(p));\nconsole.log(\"words:\", trie.words().join(\" \"));</code></pre></figure></div></div><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>search geek: true\nsearch gee: false\nsearch cut: true\nsearch dog: false\nstartsWith ge: true\nstartsWith do: false\nwords: bad bat cat cut geek geeks</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">Time O(L) per insert / search / startsWith for a word of length L</span><span class=\"chip\">Space O(total characters × alphabet) for the trie</span></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Pitfall</div><p>Returning <code>true</code> from <code>search</code> as soon as the walk succeeds finds <code>\"gee\"</code> even though only <code>\"geek\"</code> was inserted. The final <code>isEndOfWord</code> check is what separates <em>search</em> from <em>startsWith</em>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-trash\"></i>Delete</h3>\n<p>Deleting must remove the word without breaking any other word that shares its path. <code>geeks</code> and <code>geek</code> share <code>g-e-e-k</code>; deleting <code>geeks</code> may remove only the <code>s</code> node, and deleting <code>geek</code> must only clear a flag.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Example</div><p><strong>Input:</strong> <code>insert geeks, geek, bad, bat, cat, cut; delete(\"geeks\")</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>search(\"geeks\") = false, search(\"geek\") = true</code><br><strong>Input:</strong> <code>delete(\"bad\")</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>search(\"bad\") = false, search(\"bat\") = true</code></p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Idea</div><p>Write a recursive function that walks down to the last character. On the way <strong>back up</strong>: clear <code>isEndOfWord</code> at the last node; then delete a node only if it is <strong>not</strong> the end of another word <strong>and</strong> has no children. Deletion stops at the first node that is still needed.</p></div>\n<div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">1</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Recurse to the node of the last character</div><div class=\"step-desc\"><code>depth == len(key)</code>: unmark <code>isEndOfWord</code>.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">2</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Is this node now useless?</div><div class=\"step-desc\">No children and not the end of any word → return <code>null</code> so the parent drops its pointer.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">3</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Walk back up</div><div class=\"step-desc\">Each parent re-checks: if it is not a word end and has no children left, it is removed as well. The first node that still serves another word stops the chain.</div></div></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>The three cases: remove a leaf, stop at a shared node, remove a whole chain</figcaption><pre><code>delete(\"geeks\")              delete(\"bad\")                 delete(\"geek\")\ng-e-e-k*-s*                  b-a-d*                        g-e-e-k*\n         └ unmark s,         a-t*                                  └ unmark k; k is a leaf → remove\n           s has no          └ unmark d; d is a leaf        e has no children now → remove\n           children →          → remove d; a still has     e → remove, g → remove\n           remove s            child t → stop              root stays (it also has b, c)\nk keeps its * — geek lives on</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"tabs\"><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Python\"><figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>class TrieNode:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.child = [None] * 26\n        self.is_end_of_word = False\n\nclass Trie:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.root = TrieNode()\n\n    def insert(self, key):\n        node = self.root\n        for ch in key:\n            i = ord(ch) - ord('a')\n            if node.child[i] is None:\n                node.child[i] = TrieNode()\n            node = node.child[i]\n        node.is_end_of_word = True\n\n    def _walk(self, key):\n        node = self.root\n        for ch in key:\n            node = node.child[ord(ch) - ord('a')]\n            if node is None:\n                return None\n        return node\n\n    def search(self, key):\n        node = self._walk(key)\n        return node is not None and node.is_end_of_word\n\n    def starts_with(self, prefix):\n        return self._walk(prefix) is not None\n\n    def delete(self, key):\n        def _delete(node, depth):                       # returns the node to keep, or None to drop it\n            if node is None:\n                return None\n            if depth == len(key):\n                node.is_end_of_word = False             # the word itself is gone …\n            else:\n                i = ord(key[depth]) - ord('a')\n                node.child[i] = _delete(node.child[i], depth + 1)\n            if node.is_end_of_word or any(c is not None for c in node.child):\n                return node                             # … but this node still serves other words\n            return None                                 # useless node: unlink it\n        self.root = _delete(self.root, 0) or TrieNode()\n\n    def words(self):\n        out = []\n        def dfs(node, prefix):\n            if node.is_end_of_word:\n                out.append(prefix)\n            for i, c in enumerate(node.child):\n                if c is not None:\n                    dfs(c, prefix + chr(ord('a') + i))\n        dfs(self.root, \"\")\n        return out\n\nt = Trie()\nfor w in [\"geeks\", \"geek\", \"bad\", \"bat\", \"cat\", \"cut\"]:\n    t.insert(w)\nb = lambda v: \"true\" if v else \"false\"\nt.delete(\"geeks\"); print(f\"after delete(geeks): geeks={b(t.search('geeks'))} geek={b(t.search('geek'))}\")\nt.delete(\"bad\");   print(f\"after delete(bad): bad={b(t.search('bad'))} bat={b(t.search('bat'))}\")\nt.delete(\"geek\");  print(f\"after delete(geek): geek={b(t.search('geek'))} startsWith(ge)={b(t.starts_with('ge'))}\")\nprint(\"words:\", \" \".join(t.words()))</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"C++\"><figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\nusing namespace std;\n\nstruct TrieNode {\n    TrieNode* child[26] = {};\n    bool isEndOfWord = false;\n    bool hasChild() const { for (TrieNode* c : child) if (c) return true; return false; }\n};\n\nclass Trie {\n    TrieNode* root = new TrieNode();\n    TrieNode* walk(const string&amp; key) const {\n        TrieNode* node = root;\n        for (char ch : key) { node = node-&gt;child[ch - 'a']; if (!node) return nullptr; }\n        return node;\n    }\n    static TrieNode* remove(TrieNode* node, const string&amp; key, size_t depth) {   // node to keep, or nullptr\n        if (!node) return nullptr;\n        if (depth == key.size()) {\n            node-&gt;isEndOfWord = false;                              // the word itself is gone …\n        } else {\n            int i = key[depth] - 'a';\n            node-&gt;child[i] = remove(node-&gt;child[i], key, depth + 1);\n        }\n        if (node-&gt;isEndOfWord || node-&gt;hasChild()) return node;     // … but this node still serves other words\n        delete node;                                                // useless node: unlink it\n        return nullptr;\n    }\n    void dfs(TrieNode* node, string&amp; prefix, vector&lt;string&gt;&amp; out) const {\n        if (node-&gt;isEndOfWord) out.push_back(prefix);\n        for (int i = 0; i &lt; 26; i++)\n            if (node-&gt;child[i]) { prefix.push_back('a' + i); dfs(node-&gt;child[i], prefix, out); prefix.pop_back(); }\n    }\npublic:\n    void insert(const string&amp; key) {\n        TrieNode* node = root;\n        for (char ch : key) {\n            int i = ch - 'a';\n            if (!node-&gt;child[i]) node-&gt;child[i] = new TrieNode();\n            node = node-&gt;child[i];\n        }\n        node-&gt;isEndOfWord = true;\n    }\n    bool search(const string&amp; key) const { TrieNode* n = walk(key); return n &amp;&amp; n-&gt;isEndOfWord; }\n    bool startsWith(const string&amp; p) const { return walk(p) != nullptr; }\n    void erase(const string&amp; key) { root = remove(root, key, 0); if (!root) root = new TrieNode(); }\n    vector&lt;string&gt; words() const { vector&lt;string&gt; out; string p; dfs(root, p, out); return out; }\n};\n\nconst char* b(bool v) { return v ? \"true\" : \"false\"; }\n\nint main() {\n    Trie t;\n    for (string w : {\"geeks\", \"geek\", \"bad\", \"bat\", \"cat\", \"cut\"}) t.insert(w);\n    t.erase(\"geeks\"); cout &lt;&lt; \"after delete(geeks): geeks=\" &lt;&lt; b(t.search(\"geeks\")) &lt;&lt; \" geek=\" &lt;&lt; b(t.search(\"geek\")) &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    t.erase(\"bad\");   cout &lt;&lt; \"after delete(bad): bad=\" &lt;&lt; b(t.search(\"bad\")) &lt;&lt; \" bat=\" &lt;&lt; b(t.search(\"bat\")) &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    t.erase(\"geek\");  cout &lt;&lt; \"after delete(geek): geek=\" &lt;&lt; b(t.search(\"geek\")) &lt;&lt; \" startsWith(ge)=\" &lt;&lt; b(t.startsWith(\"ge\")) &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"words:\";\n    for (const string&amp; w : t.words()) cout &lt;&lt; \" \" &lt;&lt; w;\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Java\"><figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.List;\n\npublic class Main {\n    static class TrieNode {\n        TrieNode[] child = new TrieNode[26];\n        boolean isEndOfWord;\n        boolean hasChild() { for (TrieNode c : child) if (c != null) return true; return false; }\n    }\n\n    static class Trie {\n        TrieNode root = new TrieNode();\n\n        void insert(String key) {\n            TrieNode node = root;\n            for (char ch : key.toCharArray()) {\n                int i = ch - 'a';\n                if (node.child[i] == null) node.child[i] = new TrieNode();\n                node = node.child[i];\n            }\n            node.isEndOfWord = true;\n        }\n        TrieNode walk(String key) {\n            TrieNode node = root;\n            for (char ch : key.toCharArray()) { node = node.child[ch - 'a']; if (node == null) return null; }\n            return node;\n        }\n        boolean search(String key) { TrieNode n = walk(key); return n != null &amp;&amp; n.isEndOfWord; }\n        boolean startsWith(String p) { return walk(p) != null; }\n\n        void delete(String key) { root = delete(root, key, 0); if (root == null) root = new TrieNode(); }\n        TrieNode delete(TrieNode node, String key, int depth) {           // node to keep, or null to drop it\n            if (node == null) return null;\n            if (depth == key.length()) {\n                node.isEndOfWord = false;                                 // the word itself is gone …\n            } else {\n                int i = key.charAt(depth) - 'a';\n                node.child[i] = delete(node.child[i], key, depth + 1);\n            }\n            if (node.isEndOfWord || node.hasChild()) return node;         // … but this node still serves other words\n            return null;                                                  // useless node: unlink it\n        }\n        List&lt;String&gt; words() { List&lt;String&gt; out = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;(); dfs(root, new StringBuilder(), out); return out; }\n        void dfs(TrieNode node, StringBuilder prefix, List&lt;String&gt; out) {\n            if (node.isEndOfWord) out.add(prefix.toString());\n            for (int i = 0; i &lt; 26; i++)\n                if (node.child[i] != null) {\n                    prefix.append((char) ('a' + i)); dfs(node.child[i], prefix, out); prefix.setLength(prefix.length() - 1);\n                }\n        }\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Trie t = new Trie();\n        for (String w : new String[]{\"geeks\", \"geek\", \"bad\", \"bat\", \"cat\", \"cut\"}) t.insert(w);\n        t.delete(\"geeks\"); System.out.println(\"after delete(geeks): geeks=\" + t.search(\"geeks\") + \" geek=\" + t.search(\"geek\"));\n        t.delete(\"bad\");   System.out.println(\"after delete(bad): bad=\" + t.search(\"bad\") + \" bat=\" + t.search(\"bat\"));\n        t.delete(\"geek\");  System.out.println(\"after delete(geek): geek=\" + t.search(\"geek\") + \" startsWith(ge)=\" + t.startsWith(\"ge\"));\n        System.out.println(\"words: \" + String.join(\" \", t.words()));\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"JavaScript\"><figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>class TrieNode {\n    constructor() { this.child = new Array(26).fill(null); this.isEndOfWord = false; }\n    hasChild() { return this.child.some(c =&gt; c !== null); }\n}\n\nclass Trie {\n    constructor() { this.root = new TrieNode(); }\n    insert(key) {\n        let node = this.root;\n        for (const ch of key) {\n            const i = ch.charCodeAt(0) - 97;\n            if (node.child[i] === null) node.child[i] = new TrieNode();\n            node = node.child[i];\n        }\n        node.isEndOfWord = true;\n    }\n    walk(key) {\n        let node = this.root;\n        for (const ch of key) { node = node.child[ch.charCodeAt(0) - 97]; if (node === null) return null; }\n        return node;\n    }\n    search(key) { const n = this.walk(key); return n !== null &amp;&amp; n.isEndOfWord; }\n    startsWith(p) { return this.walk(p) !== null; }\n\n    delete(key) {\n        const rec = (node, depth) =&gt; {                              // node to keep, or null to drop it\n            if (node === null) return null;\n            if (depth === key.length) {\n                node.isEndOfWord = false;                           // the word itself is gone …\n            } else {\n                const i = key.charCodeAt(depth) - 97;\n                node.child[i] = rec(node.child[i], depth + 1);\n            }\n            if (node.isEndOfWord || node.hasChild()) return node;   // … but this node still serves other words\n            return null;                                            // useless node: unlink it\n        };\n        this.root = rec(this.root, 0) ?? new TrieNode();\n    }\n    words() {\n        const out = [];\n        const dfs = (node, prefix) =&gt; {\n            if (node.isEndOfWord) out.push(prefix);\n            node.child.forEach((c, i) =&gt; { if (c !== null) dfs(c, prefix + String.fromCharCode(97 + i)); });\n        };\n        dfs(this.root, \"\");\n        return out;\n    }\n}\n\nconst t = new Trie();\nfor (const w of [\"geeks\", \"geek\", \"bad\", \"bat\", \"cat\", \"cut\"]) t.insert(w);\nt.delete(\"geeks\"); console.log(`after delete(geeks): geeks=${t.search(\"geeks\")} geek=${t.search(\"geek\")}`);\nt.delete(\"bad\");   console.log(`after delete(bad): bad=${t.search(\"bad\")} bat=${t.search(\"bat\")}`);\nt.delete(\"geek\");  console.log(`after delete(geek): geek=${t.search(\"geek\")} startsWith(ge)=${t.startsWith(\"ge\")}`);\nconsole.log(\"words:\", t.words().join(\" \"));</code></pre></figure></div></div><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>after delete(geeks): geeks=false geek=true\nafter delete(bad): bad=false bat=true\nafter delete(geek): geek=false startsWith(ge)=false\nwords: bat cat cut</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">Time O(L) — one pass down and one pass back up</span><span class=\"chip\">Space O(L) recursion depth</span></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Pitfall</div><p>Only clearing <code>isEndOfWord</code> (\"lazy delete\") keeps <code>search</code> correct but leaves dead branches in memory and makes <code>startsWith(\"ge\")</code> wrongly return <code>true</code> after every <code>ge…</code> word is gone. Deleting a node that is still a word end or still has children breaks other words — check both conditions before unlinking.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-table\"></i>Count distinct rows in a binary matrix</h3>\n<p>Given a binary matrix (every entry <code>0</code> or <code>1</code>), count how many <strong>distinct</strong> rows it has.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Example</div><p><strong>Input:</strong> <code>matrix = [[1,0,0], [1,1,1], [1,0,0], [1,1,1]]</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>2</code></p></div>\n<p><strong>Naive:</strong> for every row, compare it with every earlier row; count it if it matches none. <code>R</code> rows, each compared with up to <code>R</code> others, <code>C</code> entries per comparison.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def count_distinct_rows(matrix):\n    res = 0\n    for i, row in enumerate(matrix):\n        if all(row != matrix[j] for j in range(i)):     # matches no earlier row → new\n            res += 1\n    return res\n\nprint(count_distinct_rows([[1, 0, 0], [1, 1, 1], [1, 0, 0], [1, 1, 1]]))</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>2</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">Time O(R² · C)</span><span class=\"chip\">Space O(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Idea</div><p>Treat every row as a <em>word</em> over the alphabet <code>{0, 1}</code> and insert it into a <strong>binary trie</strong> (two children per node). Insertion of a row that is already present ends at a node that is already marked — so <code>insert</code> can report whether the row was new. Every row is inserted once: <code>O(R · C)</code> in total.</p></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>A binary trie of the rows: equal rows walk the same path and hit the same leaf</figcaption><pre><code>rows: 1 0 0 / 1 1 1 / 1 0 0 / 1 1 1\n\n         (root)\n           │ 1\n           ●\n      0 ┌──┴──┐ 1\n        ●     ●\n      0 │     │ 1\n        ●*    ●*          two marked leaves → 2 distinct rows</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"tabs\"><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Python\"><figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>class Node:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.child = [None, None]          # child[0] for a 0, child[1] for a 1\n        self.is_end = False\n\ndef insert_row(root, row):                 # True if the row was not present before\n    node = root\n    for bit in row:\n        if node.child[bit] is None:\n            node.child[bit] = Node()\n        node = node.child[bit]\n    if node.is_end:\n        return False                       # an identical row ended here already\n    node.is_end = True\n    return True\n\ndef count_distinct_rows(matrix):\n    root = Node()\n    return sum(1 for row in matrix if insert_row(root, row))\n\nmatrix = [[1, 0, 0], [1, 1, 1], [1, 0, 0], [1, 1, 1]]\nprint(\"distinct rows:\", count_distinct_rows(matrix))</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"C++\"><figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\nusing namespace std;\n\nstruct Node {\n    Node* child[2] = {nullptr, nullptr};   // child[0] for a 0, child[1] for a 1\n    bool isEnd = false;\n};\n\nbool insertRow(Node* root, const vector&lt;int&gt;&amp; row) {   // true if the row was not present before\n    Node* node = root;\n    for (int bit : row) {\n        if (!node-&gt;child[bit]) node-&gt;child[bit] = new Node();\n        node = node-&gt;child[bit];\n    }\n    if (node-&gt;isEnd) return false;                     // an identical row ended here already\n    node-&gt;isEnd = true;\n    return true;\n}\n\nint countDistinctRows(const vector&lt;vector&lt;int&gt;&gt;&amp; matrix) {\n    Node* root = new Node();\n    int res = 0;\n    for (const auto&amp; row : matrix)\n        if (insertRow(root, row)) res++;\n    return res;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    vector&lt;vector&lt;int&gt;&gt; matrix = {{1, 0, 0}, {1, 1, 1}, {1, 0, 0}, {1, 1, 1}};\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"distinct rows: \" &lt;&lt; countDistinctRows(matrix) &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Java\"><figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    static class Node {\n        Node[] child = new Node[2];        // child[0] for a 0, child[1] for a 1\n        boolean isEnd;\n    }\n\n    static boolean insertRow(Node root, int[] row) {   // true if the row was not present before\n        Node node = root;\n        for (int bit : row) {\n            if (node.child[bit] == null) node.child[bit] = new Node();\n            node = node.child[bit];\n        }\n        if (node.isEnd) return false;                  // an identical row ended here already\n        node.isEnd = true;\n        return true;\n    }\n\n    static int countDistinctRows(int[][] matrix) {\n        Node root = new Node();\n        int res = 0;\n        for (int[] row : matrix)\n            if (insertRow(root, row)) res++;\n        return res;\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int[][] matrix = {{1, 0, 0}, {1, 1, 1}, {1, 0, 0}, {1, 1, 1}};\n        System.out.println(\"distinct rows: \" + countDistinctRows(matrix));\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"JavaScript\"><figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>class Node {\n    constructor() { this.child = [null, null]; this.isEnd = false; }   // child[0] for a 0, child[1] for a 1\n}\n\nfunction insertRow(root, row) {               // true if the row was not present before\n    let node = root;\n    for (const bit of row) {\n        if (node.child[bit] === null) node.child[bit] = new Node();\n        node = node.child[bit];\n    }\n    if (node.isEnd) return false;             // an identical row ended here already\n    node.isEnd = true;\n    return true;\n}\n\nfunction countDistinctRows(matrix) {\n    const root = new Node();\n    let res = 0;\n    for (const row of matrix) if (insertRow(root, row)) res++;\n    return res;\n}\n\nconst matrix = [[1, 0, 0], [1, 1, 1], [1, 0, 0], [1, 1, 1]];\nconsole.log(\"distinct rows:\", countDistinctRows(matrix));</code></pre></figure></div></div><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>distinct rows: 2</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">Time O(R · C)</span><span class=\"chip\">Space O(R · C) for the trie in the worst case</span></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Alternative</div><p>A hash set of rows (as tuples / strings) also solves this in <code>O(R · C)</code> expected time and is shorter to write. The trie answers two things the set cannot: it can list the distinct rows in <strong>sorted order</strong>, and its memory is shared across rows with equal prefixes.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-message-question\"></i>Interview Q&amp;A</h3>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>When would you choose a trie over a hash map?</summary><div><p>When the workload needs <strong>prefix</strong> operations — autocomplete, \"does any key start with…\", longest-prefix match (IP routing) — or <strong>sorted</strong> enumeration of the keys. For pure exact-match lookups a hash map is faster and uses far less memory.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is the space complexity of a trie, and how can it be reduced?</summary><div><p>Worst case <code>O(N · L · A)</code> for <code>N</code> words of length <code>L</code> over an alphabet of size <code>A</code>, because every node reserves <code>A</code> child slots. Reduce it with a hash map or sorted list of children per node (only the children that exist), with a <strong>compressed trie / radix tree</strong> that merges chains of single-child nodes, or with a <strong>ternary search tree</strong>.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How do you implement autocomplete with a trie?</summary><div><p>Walk down the prefix (<code>O(P)</code>); if the walk fails there are no completions. Otherwise do a DFS from that node, appending characters, and emit every node with <code>isEndOfWord</code>. The DFS visits the children in alphabetical order, so the suggestions come out sorted. Store a <em>count</em> or <em>top-k</em> list in each node to rank suggestions without a full traversal.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why does a trie give lexicographic order for free?</summary><div><p>A pre-order traversal that visits the children in index order (<code>a</code> before <code>b</code> before <code>c</code>…) reaches every word end in dictionary order, because a shorter word is a prefix of the longer ones below it and siblings are visited alphabetically. This is also why <code>words()</code> above prints <code>bad bat cat cut geek geeks</code>.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What goes wrong if delete just clears the end-of-word flag?</summary><div><p>Searches stay correct, but memory for dead branches is never reclaimed, and <code>startsWith</code> returns <code>true</code> for prefixes that no longer lead to any word. Proper deletion removes nodes bottom-up while they are unmarked and childless, stopping at the first node another word still uses.</p></div></details></section>"}